  "worked_solution": "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{({n}^{3}+1)({n}^{3}-1)}{n(2-n)}=\\frac{{n}^{6}-1}{2n-{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{6}-1}{2n-{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{6}-1}{2n-{n}^{2}} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{n}^{6}}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2n}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{({n}^{4}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2}{n}-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{4}-0}{0-1} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence diverges.",
